s up two, so the expected margin stays modest rather than decisive.", "watch": "Any second independent poll or a shift in the national midterm environment could move a race this thin in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Evans (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 8th District is an even partisan split, and first-term Republican Gabe Evans is defending the seat against Democrat Manny Rutinel in one of the House's closest contests. Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, and Sabato's Crystal Ball call it a Tossup, while the Economist, Split Ticket, Fox News, and several model-based raters see a Lean or Likely Democratic flip. The only public poll, from a Republican-leaning firm in mid-September, showed Evans ahead 48-46, and local coverage describes the race as essentially tied.\n\nMoney is flowing on both sides: Evans holds a large cash-on-hand edge, but outside groups have spent roughly $3.5 million supporting Rutinel against under $500,000 backing Evans. Prediction markets give the Democrat a 75 to 86 percent chance, reflecting the favorable midterm climate in a district with no partisan lean.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7882, "margin": 4.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7652, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7649, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7806, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7027, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6862, "margin": 3.5}]}